WEBVTT

1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,454
Segmentation separates systems so a mistake, compromise, or overload in one area does not automatically affect everything else.

2
00:00:08,454 --> 00:00:14,064
A flat network allows too much unnecessary communication and increases blast radius.

3
00:00:14,064 --> 00:00:27,744
Segmentation introduces zones: user networks, server networks, management networks, guest wireless, production, development, sensitive environments, cloud networks, and third-party connection areas.

4
00:00:27,744 --> 00:00:32,663
Each zone should have a purpose and a reason for the communication it allows.

5
00:00:32,663 --> 00:00:40,272
A trust boundary is the place where communication should be controlled, inspected, logged, or explicitly approved.

6
00:00:40,272 --> 00:00:49,571
Boundaries often sit at firewalls, access control lists, security groups, VPN gateways, identity-aware proxies, or cloud routing controls.

7
00:00:49,571 --> 00:01:01,330
A boundary is useful when it reflects real risk: data sensitivity, business function, administrative privilege, partner access, internet exposure, or operational criticality.

8
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:05,787
It is less useful when it exists only as a diagram that no one operates.

9
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:08,631
Segmentation should support the business.

10
00:01:08,631 --> 00:01:18,622
A payroll system, guest wireless network, building control network, development environment, and production database do not need the same trust model.

11
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:24,463
Some systems need tightly controlled access, while others need broad user reachability.

12
00:01:24,463 --> 00:01:29,997
Good segmentation creates clear communication paths and denies unnecessary paths.

13
00:01:29,997 --> 00:01:38,758
It should also support logging, troubleshooting, and incident response so teams can understand what is happening across boundaries.

14
00:01:38,758 --> 00:01:42,755
Avoid designs that are so complex teams cannot maintain them.

15
00:01:42,755 --> 00:01:51,209
Overly complicated segmentation can lead to emergency exceptions, undocumented firewall rules, and fragile troubleshooting.

16
00:01:51,209 --> 00:01:56,665
Start with critical zones, known data flows, and high-value administrative paths.

17
00:01:56,665 --> 00:01:59,663
Review the design as the environment changes.

18
00:01:59,663 --> 00:02:07,502
Effective segmentation is understandable, enforceable, and aligned with ownership, monitoring, and change control.
